# Carrier Wound #20: JSON Lanes Scrape pi's Lossy Text Channel

The verifier lane reported `adapter_unavailable` and the capture was forged. The
agent had actually produced a clean verdict — the carrier threw it away and
manufactured an outage out of a healthy run.

## Mechanism

A franchise of the [[polite-fakes-franchise-wound]]: the test doubles were
politer than production because they never reproduced pi's **print-mode
semantics**.

pi has two output channels:

1. **text mode** (default `-p`): writes ONLY the LAST assistant message's raw
   text, and ONLY when `role === "assistant"` and `stopReason` is not
   `error`/`aborted`. If the run ends on a tool call, an error, or an abort,
   stdout is **empty**. It is a human channel, not a machine contract.
2. **`--mode json`**: writes the full JSONL event stream — a `session` header
   line, then `agent_start` / `turn_start` / `message_*`, terminated by
   `agent_end` carrying `messages: AgentMessage[]`. The verdict lives in the last
   assistant message's `content[{type:"text"}].text` (next to a
   `{type:"thinking"}` block).

The lane ran pi in **text mode** and treated stdout as the verdict. When a
verifier turn ended via a tool call (or the agent thought-then-stopped without a
trailing text echo), stdout came back empty. The Sandbox command exits with pi's
status (`exit "$pi_status"`), so `result.success` was still true — but the
JSON normalize found nothing, the old code set `pi_status=65` to signal that,
and the runner's `if (!result.success) throw` fired. A committed, pushed receipt
was discarded and re-cast as `adapter_unavailable`. **The agent succeeded; the
carrier reported an infrastructure failure.**

This is the sixth carrier-wound class confirmed via read-consistency failures
(see [[dream-pilot-progress-2026-06-11]] run 17): the carrier kept inventing
errors that the primary source never produced.

## Fix — Define the Error Out of Existence

> "The best way to eliminate exception handling complexity is to define your APIs
> so that there are no exceptions to handle: define errors out of existence...
> its work is already done, so it can simply return. There is no longer an error
> case to report." — A Philosophy of Software Design (Ousterhout), Ch. 10

Four moves, all in the carrier:

1. **JSON lanes invoke `pi --mode json`** (gated on
   `LANE_OUTPUT_MEDIA_TYPE === "application/json"`).
   `normalizeAgentLaneJsonOutput` parses the event stream, walks `agent_end` →
   last assistant message, and extracts the verdict from its text (fence/prose
   tolerant). A whole-buffer fallback is guarded behind `!looksLikeEventStream`
   so it can never scrape the `session` header as a result.
2. **Honest agent failures throw a typed `AgentLaneAgentError`** carrying the
   real `stopReason` + `errorMessage` when the run ended `error`/`aborted` — not
   a generic "no JSON".
3. **Normalization success is tracked in `output_normalized`, separate from
   `pi_status`.** A failed normalize records `outputNormalization.normalized =
   false` (with `reason` + `agentStopReason`) on the receipt and NO LONGER
   clobbers the exit code into a fake `65`.
4. **Delete the `if (!result.success) throw` in the Sandbox runner.** The
   receipt's own `status` (`"completed" | "failed"`) is the single source of
   truth — every consumer already gates on it. The `laneCompleted` flag in the
   receipt builder is now `piExitOk && outputNormalization.normalized !== false`.

Adapter-side (`cloudflare-agent-lane-adapters.ts`) failure messages now name the
real cause — "agent produced no parseable verdict (reason: ..., stopReason:
...)", "receipt kind/lane mismatch", or the raw status — instead of a generic
outage. The closed `CapabilityDenialCode` enum was NOT touched: no new blocker
code, just honest messages.

## Schema

Added optional `outputNormalization` to `AgentLaneReceiptSchema` (NOT the draft
schema — the `prompt: ArtifactPinSchema` + `realAgent` fields disambiguate):

```ts
outputNormalization: z
  .object({
    agentStopReason: z.string().nullable().default(null),
    normalized: z.boolean(),
    reason: z.string().nullable().default(null),
  })
  .optional()
```

Optional + additive — no ripple through existing receipts.

## Chaos Receipts (would have caught it)

`tests/integration/cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.test.ts` — 21 passing. The key
move: a `buildPiJsonEventStream()` fixture that faithfully reproduces pi's
`--mode json` JSONL (session header + agent_start + turn_start + message_start +
agent_end; assistant carries a `{type:"thinking"}` + `{type:"text"}` block;
error/abort omits the text block and adds `errorMessage`). This is the hostile
double — the real captured stream shape, not a polite stub.

- verdict recovery from `agent_end` assistant text
- fenced verdict buried in prose
- `AgentLaneAgentError` on `error` (asserts `.agentStopReason` + `.message`)
- `AgentLaneAgentError` on `aborted`
- **"never returns the session header"**: strip the text block → expect throw
  "Agent lane output did not contain a complete JSON value." (the wound itself)
- legacy bare-JSON + chatty single-line fallbacks
- sandbox node script end-to-end via `execFileSync`: success (exit 0, verdict
  written, `{agentStopReason:null,normalized:true,reason:null}`), agent-error
  (exit≠0, `reason:"agent_error"`), no-parseable (`reason:"no_parseable_output"`)
- `buildPiAgentLaneCommand` asserts `pi_mode_args="--mode json"`,
  `!command.includes("pi_status=65")`, `exit "$pi_status"`, the media-type gate,
  and `export LANE_OUTPUT_NORMALIZATION_PATH`.

## Verification

```bash
pnpm typecheck
pnpm check
pnpm vitest run tests/integration/cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.test.ts \
  tests/integration/cloudflare-agent-lane-adapters.test.ts \
  tests/integration/admitted-agent-lane-runtime.test.ts \
  tests/integration/agent-lane-token-cost-accounting.test.ts
```

typecheck + check clean; 21 + 18 = 39 lane/adapter tests pass. Committed
`20b8728` through the lefthook pre-commit gate (format + lint + typecheck).

## Burn-In Gate

Per the Phase 2 D3 acceptance bar, the 10-run count stays at **zero**. This fix
must be deployed and a fresh dream-shape run must reach `captured` **observed
live on deployed code** before the burn-in restarts. Any failure is a carrier
failure: fix the class, add the chaos test that would have caught it, reset the
count.
